00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:15.669
South of San Francisco, in Menlo Park, the
heart of Silicon Valley, lie a series of unremarkable

00:00:15.669 --> 00:00:16.810
offices.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:22.419
They have creative names like “Building
57”, “Building 40”, and wait for it!

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:25.419
none other than the Employment Development
Department!

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:31.470
There’s a plan, though, to turn this 59-acre
office park into a more-or-less fully self-contained,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.420
city called Willow Village.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.989
It’ll have a grocery store, pharmacy, retail
stores, playgrounds, hotel rooms, a community

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:42.460
center, and 1,500 housing units.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.940
Oh, and one more thing it’s 100% owned by
everyone’s favorite company.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.870
No, not Equifax.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.880
Not Comcast or Enron.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.829
Worse: Facebook.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:59.500
In just a few years, Facebook employees will
be able to work, eat, play, shop, and now,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.829
sleep, all without leaving the property of
the world’s most trustworthy company.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:08.330
Google, meanwhile, plans to build over 10,000
homes in Mountain View,

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.360
And Apple just finished the 6th most expensive
building in the world, behind Singapore’s

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.970
famous Marina Bay Sands.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.540
As companies expand their physical presence,
the line between public and private is being

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:26.070
blurred, and local governments find themselves
not governing but being governed by more powerful

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.560
corporations.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:33.490
And it all stems from one simple economic
problem.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.670
College majors are generally fairly constant.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.420
About the same percentage of students choose
physics, or psychology, or English from one

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.890
year to the next.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:43.890
Generally.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.810
Up until 2008.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:52.440
The financial crisis had an immediate, and
so far, it seems, permanent effect on colleges.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.350
When the sun is out and jobs are abundant,
like right now, the relative value of more

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.340
education is lower.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.650
Your goal is to get employed as fast as possible
and ride the wave.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.429
When times are tough, the opportunity cost
is much lower - it’s harder to get a job,

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:09.979
keep it, or get promoted.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:15.870
So, you do the opposite - go back to school
and pick the most marketable major available.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.260
And that’s exactly what happened.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.189
The Great Recession changed the entire purpose
of education.

00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:26.950
The number one reason people chose to go to
college in 2006 was “to learn more about

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.560
things that interest me”.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.480
In 2009, it was “to get a better job”,
and has stayed that way ever since.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.890
First, students left the humanities.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:42.439
In the ten years after the recession, U.S.
English degrees dropped 22%, Education 19%,

00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:45.380
and Philosophy or religious studies, 15.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:51.409
Meanwhile, Health-related degrees have doubled,
Biological and biomedical sciences have increased

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:54.790
61%, and engineering, 60%.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.150
Computer Science, specifically, has absolutely
exploded.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.860
It’s not hard to see why.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:06.730
If you find yourself getting mediocre grades,
often missing class, and with totally unremarkable

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.450
skills, congratulations!

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:14.819
As an average U.S. computer programmer, you
can expect to make $50-55,000 a year, right

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:16.079
out of college.

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:19.340
No experience or graduate degree required.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:25.560
In other words, to earn more than 64% of the
country, all you need to do is stay in school

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.709
for 1,460 days.

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:32.299
If you keep doing average work, don’t go
back to school, but manage to keep this up

00:03:32.299 --> 00:03:35.170
for a few more years, congratulations again!

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.409
Now you have experience.

00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:43.420
You’re looking at a median salary of $105,000,
making more than 88% of the country.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:48.680
Now, I’m no economist but, something about
this doesn’t seem quite right.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.189
Students are picking majors based on their
career prospects now more than ever, salaries

00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:57.430
are off the charts and credentials, quick
to obtain, which means, the market should

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.700
be absolutely flooded with Computer Science
students, increasing supply, allowing salaries

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.739
to fall, and soon reaching an equilibrium.

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:10.610
The problem is that demand for Computer Science
has grown really quickly, even compared to

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.579
other engineering majors, but universities
are slow.

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:18.459
If they could snap their fingers and produce
more professors, they would.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.970
But the lag time is incredibly long.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.120
If you’re a new high school graduate who
sees demand for Computer Science teachers,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.040
it’ll take at least nine years to get a
bachelor’s, then master’s degree, then

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.140
doctorate, and by then, things have may have
changed.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.820
More importantly, if you can make six figures
with just a bachelors degree working for a

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.920
company, why spent five more years only to
make the same or lower salary?

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:46.820
Schools are competing for professors with
the very companies whose high demand for graduates

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.590
has created the problem in the first place.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:54.390
They can only increase class sizes so much
before it starts to hurt the staff-to-student

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.000
ratio, which lowers the school’s ranking.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.280
At the University of Washington in Seattle,
only a third of students who apply to CS programs

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.420
are accepted.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.480
To be clear, that means two-thirds of freshman
and sophomore students who are already accepted

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.600
to the school can’t get into their desired
major because it’s full.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:20.450
As of 2017, about 530,000 open jobs are fighting
for about 60,000 new graduates a year.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:26.000
Employees, even mediocre ones, but especially
the most talented among them, have most of

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.510
the leverage here.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:32.100
Not only do they always have the promise of
high salaries, signing bonuses, and stock

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.660
options elsewhere,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.630
But because tech companies are so concentrated
in Silicon Valley, changing jobs is really

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:39.740
easy.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.970
You won’t have to move to a new state, or
climate, maybe not even a new house.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:51.350
Apple Park is a 15-mile drive from Stanford,
Tesla Headquarters, 3 miles, Facebook, 9,

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:52.850
and Google, 7.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.390
Software companies, therefore, have about
the same turnover rate as McDonald’s.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:04.320
The average Tesla employee, for example, lasts
2.1 years, and three is considered quite good.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.280
So tech companies have to go out of their
way to make workers happy.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:15.060
That means free food, gym memberships, cellphones,
unlimited vacation days, laundry, bikes, childcare,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.760
yoga, nap rooms, luxury lockers, and massages.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:23.680
Google, Apple, and Facebook will even pay
for women who aren’t ready to have children

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:24.890
to freeze their eggs.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.380
Still, it’s not enough.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.760
Especially for Facebook, whose poor reputation
has reportedly made it much harder to attract

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:32.760
talent.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:38.810
According to CNBC, nearly every applicant
Facebook offered a job to in 2016 accepted,

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.360
while only about half do today.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.170
The ultimate way to keep employees, though,
is to involve yourself in every aspect of

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:47.170
their lives.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:51.580
It’s much harder to leave a company when
it owns your house and those of your friends

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:52.580
and family.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:57.440
Already, Facebook offers at least $10,000
to live near the office.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.680
Apple and Google, meanwhile, drive employees
who live in San Francisco to work in luxury,

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.760
WiFi-equipped busses.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.500
For the employee and the company, all of these
things make sense.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.490
But they often come at the expense of the
community as a whole.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:17.950
By using municipal bus stops to load and unload,
they use public infrastructure without supporting

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.520
or improving the quality of public transportation.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.030
They’ve also made housing unaffordable,
not only around their campuses but in neighborhoods

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:28.050
as far away as San Francisco.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:33.610
And, as their physical presence grows, they
begin taking over some of the roles of government.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:38.800
In 2014, for example, Facebook paid for a
new police station next to its campus, along

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.780
with the $170,000 salary of an officer.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.300
That should be a red flag.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:49.500
It’s time for the country to reevaluate
the power big companies have over government,

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.150
and maybe not the time for Facebook to try
replacing the US Dollar.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:59.140
